 148: ## 50. The four-pencil tempo shield
 149: 
 150: ### 50.1 One exact Attacker pair
 151: 
 152: From `Q_ax`, Attacker plays
 153: 
 154: `p=(0,1)`, then `p'=(1,-1)`.                               (56)
 155: 
 156: Both placements are empty and adjacent to old Attacker stones, so the ordered
 157: pair is legal. The first placement promotes five R-windows through `(0,0)`
 158: and five QR-windows through `(1,0)`. The second promotes the complementary
 159: five R-windows through `(1,0)` and five QR-windows through `(0,0)`. The two
 160: new cells are not collinear, so no window contains both of them without also
 161: being one of those old pencils.
 162: 
 163: **Lemma L12.1 (exact diamond profile) [Q1, PROVEN].** At the returned
 164: Defender epoch the exact local profile is
 165: 
 166: `n_2=20, n_1=20`, with no label of higher count.             (57)
 167: 
 168: The twenty count-two labels form four five-window adjacent-pair pencils on
 169: 
 170: `q=0`, `q=1`, `q+r=0`, and `q+r=1`.                        (58)
 171: 
 172: *Proof.* Equation (56) promotes twenty of the twenty-four old count-one
 173: labels. The four unpromoted old extremes stay at count one. Each new stone
 174: lies in eighteen windows, ten of which are the promoted focal labels. Its
 175: other eight windows are new count-one labels. The two new stones share no
 176: axis, so the two sets of eight are distinct. None of the external Defender
 177: stones lies within a six-window of a new stone. Thus
 178: `n_1=4+8+8=20`, and (57) follows. The four promoted adjacent pairs are
 179: respectively
 180: 
 181: ```text
 182: (0,0),(0,1);   (1,-1),(1,0);
 183: (0,0),(1,-1);  (0,1),(1,0),
 184: ```
 185: 
 186: which gives exactly the four lines in (58).  âˆŽ
 187: 
 188: The two fixed-`q` lines are parallel, as are the two fixed-`q+r` lines. The
 189: four remaining intersections are
 190: 
 191: ```text
 192: (0,0), (0,1), (1,-1), (1,0),
 193: ```
 194: 
 195: and all four are occupied by Attacker. Therefore an empty Defender cell lies
 196: on at most one line in (58).
 197: 
 198: ### 50.2 Every immediate value is exactly two
 199: 
 200: For two adjacent Attacker cells at axis parameters `0,1`, call the five
 201: common windows the *raw pencil*. Call it *intact* when all five labels are
 202: Defender-free at count two and the witness cells at parameters `-1,2` are
 203: empty. In an intact pencil, those future triggers make the three windows with
 204: starts `-3,-2,-1` imminent, with residuals
 205: 
 206: `{-3,-2}`, `{-2,3}`, and `{3,4}`.                           (59)
 207: 
 208: Their hitting number is two: the first and third grounds are disjoint, while
 209: `{-2,3}` hits all three.
 210: 
 211: **Lemma L12.2 (four-pencil plateau) [Q1, PROVEN].** Suppose a Defender epoch
 212: has `I=empty`, every `L_23` label has count two, and it contains the four
 213: intact raw pencils in (58). Suppose also that no empty cell lies on two of
 214: their four central lines. Then every legal ordered Defender pair hands over
 215: exactly
 216: 
 217: `TEMPO=2`.                                                 (60)
 218: 
 219: *Proof.* Defender augmentation preserves the property that every surviving
 220: `L_23` label has count two, so L10.4 gives `TEMPO<=2` after every pair. One
 221: Defender cell can meet labels of at most one of the four raw pencils. Two
 222: cells therefore leave at least two pencils untouched. In particular one
 223: untouched pencil retains the legal triggers and residual family (59), so its
 224: component has `g>=2`. Equation (20) gives `TEMPO>=2`.  âˆŽ
 225: 
 226: At the epoch of L12.1, `I=empty`, all graded labels are count two, and the
 227: intersection condition was just proved. The earlier Defender cells do not
 228: damage a shield pencil: `(0,8)` lies beyond the union on `q=0`, `(-8,8)` lies
 229: beyond the union on `q+r=0`, and all other old Defender cells lie on none of
 230: the four finite pencil unions. Hence every candidate `S_T` reply has the same
 231: value two.
 232: 
 233: ### 50.3 Exact lexicographic ray
 234: 
 235: **Lemma L12.3 (left-ray tie breaker) [Q1, PROVEN].** Suppose `I=empty` at a
 236: Defender epoch, every legal ordered pair has the same `TEMPO`, and the
 237: occupied set has a unique minimum-`q` cell `(ell,8)`. Then the exact `S_T`
 238: pair is
 239: 
 240: `D@(ell-8,8), D@(ell-16,8)`.                               (61)
 241: 
 242: Afterward `(ell-16,8)` is the unique minimum-`q` occupied cell.
 243: 
 244: *Proof.* Radius-eight legality implies that no first cell can have
 245: `q<ell-8`. The cell `(ell-8,8)` is legal from `(ell,8)`. Because the old
 246: minimum-`q` cell is unique, any legal cell with `q=ell-8` must be supported
 247: from `(ell,8)`; the axial distance inequalities make `r=8` the least possible
 248: second coordinate. After that placement the same argument gives
 249: `(ell-16,8)` as the lexicographically first second cell. Since `I=empty`,
 250: every legal pair services; since all such pairs tie in the primary objective,
 251: `S_T` uses these two lexicographic minima.  âˆŽ
 252: 
 253: Immediately before the first plateau reply, `(-16,8)` is the unique
 254: minimum-`q` occupied cell. Lemmas L12.2--L12.3 therefore derive the actual
 255: reply
 256: 
 257: `D@(-24,8), D@(-32,8)`.                                   (62)
 258: 
 259: This is not an asserted filler choice: it follows from the complete
 260: `TEMPO=2` tie and the actual definition of `S_T`.
 261: 
 262: ## 51. Exact hub assembly behind the shield
 263: 
 264: ### 51.1 The five Attacker stock turns
 265: 
 266: Set
 267: 
 268: `h=(10,0)`, `u=(1,0)`, `v=(0,1)`, and `w=(1,-1)`.
 269: 
 270: On five successive Attacker turns, play
 271: 
 272: ```text
 273: U- stock:  (6,0),   (7,0)
 274: V- stock:  (10,-4), (10,-3)
 275: W  stock:  (12,-2), (13,-3)
 276: V+ stock:  (10,4),  (10,5)
 277: U+ stock:  (14,0),  (15,0).                               (63)
 278: ```
 279: 
 280: Every ordered pair is legal on the ordinary 2:2 cadence. The first placements
 281: have the following already occupied supports and distances; every second
 282: placement is adjacent to its first:
 283: 
 284: | First placement | Earlier support | Hex distance |
 285: |---|---|---:|
 286: | `(6,0)` | `(1,0)` | 5 |
 287: | `(10,-4)` | `(7,0)` | 4 |
 288: | `(12,-2)` | `(10,-3)` | 3 |
 289: | `(10,4)` | `(10,-3)` | 7 |
 290: | `(14,0)` | `(13,-3)` | 4 |
 291: 
 292: Thus all ten placements satisfy the inclusive radius-eight rule. They are
 293: also disjoint from all earlier Defender cells and from the hub `h`.
 294: 
 295: ### 51.2 No hidden high label
 296: 
 297: **Lemma L12.4 (global count-two audit) [Q1, PROVEN].** After all placements
 298: in (63), every length-six window contains at most two Attacker stones. The
 299: same is true after every initial segment of (63).
 300: 
 301: *Proof.* It is enough to inspect the final Attacker set, since intermediate
 302: sets are subsets. On fixed-`q` lines, the only groups of size above one are
 303: 
 304: ```text
 305: q=0:  r={0,1};
 306: q=1:  r={-1,0};
 307: q=10: r={-4,-3,4,5}.
 308: ```
 309: 
 310: The two pairs on `q=10` have span seven between `-3` and `4`, so a six-cell
 311: interval meets at most one pair. On fixed-`r` lines, the only larger groups
 312: are
 313: 
 314: ```text
 315: r=0:  q={0,1,6,7,14,15};
 316: r=-3: q={10,13}.
 317: ```
 318: 
 319: Every six-consecutive factor of the first set contains at most two listed
 320: coordinates. Finally, on fixed-`q+r` lines the non-singleton groups have
 321: levels
 322: 
 323: ```text
 324: 0, 1, 6, 7, 10, 14, 15,
 325: ```
 326: 
 327: and each level contains exactly two Attacker stones. These three line
 328: families are all lattice axes, proving the claim.  âˆŽ
 329: 
 330: In particular every intermediate Defender epoch has `I=empty`, and every
 331: surviving `L_23` label has count two. None of the stock cells in (63) lies on
 332: one of the shield lines `q=0`, `q=1`, `q+r=0`, or `q+r=1`, so the four raw
 333: pencils remain intact. Every candidate Defender pair is therefore servicing,
 334: and L12.2 makes its handoff value exactly two.
 335: 
 336: The unique leftmost occupied cell remains on the row `r=8`; each actual
 337: reply extends it by equation (61). Consequently the complete sequence of
 338: actual `S_T` replies is
 339: 
 340: | Attacker pair just played | Exact `S_T` reply |
 341: |---|---|
 342: | shield pair `(0,1),(1,-1)` | `(-24,8),(-32,8)` |
 343: | `(6,0),(7,0)` | `(-40,8),(-48,8)` |
 344: | `(10,-4),(10,-3)` | `(-56,8),(-64,8)` |
 345: | `(12,-2),(13,-3)` | `(-72,8),(-80,8)` |
 346: | `(10,4),(10,5)` | `(-88,8),(-96,8)` |
 347: | `(14,0),(15,0)` | `(-104,8),(-112,8)` |
 348: 
 349: Each first Defender cell is at distance exactly eight from the preceding
 350: leftmost cell, and each second is at distance exactly eight from the first.
 351: Thus the table derives both legality and the ordered `FirstStone`/
 352: `SecondStone` choices; no simultaneous-pair convention is used.
 353: 
 354: ### 51.3 The attained handoff
 355: 
 356: Let `Q_H^*` be the Attacker handoff after the last reply in the table. Define
 357: 
 358: ```text
 359: U^- = {h+t u : -4<=t<=1},
 360: U^+ = {h+t u :  0<=t<=5},
 361: V^- = {h+t v : -4<=t<=1},
 362: V^+ = {h+t v :  0<=t<=5},
 363: W   = {h+t w : -2<=t<=3}.                                 (64)
 364: ```
 365: 
 366: These are the five focal windows of L11.6, translated to `h`. Their exact
 367: Attacker supports are
 368: 
 369: ```text
 370: U^-: (6,0),(7,0);       U^+: (14,0),(15,0);
 371: V^-: (10,-4),(10,-3);   V^+: (10,4),(10,5);
 372: W:   (12,-2),(13,-3).
 373: ```
 374: 
 375: No displayed `S_T` cell belongs to a window in (64). The old anchors and the
 376: two axial-cleanup cells also lie outside them. Hence all five windows are
 377: Defender-free and have count exactly two. Their common hub `h=(10,0)` is
 378: empty. This proves Theorem R6.1.
